+# The Daily Grind
+
+Coffee shop account security with real WebAuthn passkeys and password fallback. The account page includes passkey creation, discoverable passkey sign-in, naming and removal, password updates, and recent security activity.
+
+## Run
+
+Requires Node.js 24 or newer and pnpm.
+
+```sh
+pnpm install
+pnpm start
+```
+
+Open `http://localhost:11222`. `PORT` overrides the default port. The frontend is bundled locally when the server starts; no CDN JavaScript is required.
+
+Choose **Add a passkey** to create an account or sign into an existing account, then register a passkey using the device's authenticator or password manager. Choose **My account** while signed out for password or passkey sign-in. Registering an account creates a password fallback first so removing the last passkey does not lock out the account.
+
+## Hosting and storage
+
+Passkeys require HTTPS, except on localhost. Set `PUBLIC_ORIGIN` to the exact public origin in deployment, for example `https://coffee.example.com`. Registration and authentication check that origin and its hostname as the relying party ID. Passkeys are scoped to that hostname and do not transfer to a different deployment domain.
+
+The app supports one reverse proxy hop for forwarded protocol information. Configure the proxy to replace forwarded headers and preserve the public Host header. Set `PUBLIC_ORIGIN` explicitly for hosted deployments.
+
+User accounts, public credential keys and counters, activity, and sessions persist in `data/accounts.sqlite` (excluded from Git). Use a persistent volume. `DATABASE_PATH` can override the location; create its parent directory first. Private passkey keys and biometric data never reach this server. Passwords use salted scrypt hashes. Sessions use HttpOnly, SameSite cookies, with Secure cookies on HTTPS, and rotate after sign-in. Mutation endpoints enforce a per-session CSRF token and check browser request origins.
+
+Changes to passkeys and passwords require an authenticated session verified within the last five minutes. When that expires, the interface prompts for a password or a passkey belonging to the current account. Challenges expire after five minutes and can only be consumed once. Password changes invalidate other sessions. Authentication endpoints are rate limited in process; this app uses a single Node process and SQLite.
+
+## Automated checks
+
+```sh
+pnpm test
+```
+
+Tests use an in-memory database and a software ES256 authenticator to exercise real cryptographic registration and authentication. They cover lifecycle management, reauthentication, credential ownership, CSRF, fallback sign-in, session revocation, replay, origin checks, challenge expiry, user verification, bad signatures, and public asset isolation. No manual browser verification is required or performed.
+
+The implementation uses [SimpleWebAuthn's server and browser libraries](https://simplewebauthn.dev/docs/packages/server). Google Fonts supplies the typefaces; the sidebar uses a stock Unsplash photo. The café menu and informational dialogs are illustrative; ordering, loyalty points, email delivery and email recovery are not backend integrations.
